      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^3-ax^2&=1\\\\x^2(x-a)&=1\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe roots correspond to intersections of $y=x^2(x-a)$ and $y=1$. The cubic has a double root at $0$ and a single root at $a$.\n\nFor $x\\le a$, $x^2(x-a)\\le0$, so there is no intersection with $y=1$.\n\nFor $x>a>0$, both factors $x^2$ and $x-a$ are positive and strictly increasing. Their continuous product increases from $0$ without bound, so it equals $1$ exactly once. Thus there is exactly one positive root $r$, with $r>a$.\n\nLet $a_2>a_1>0$, with corresponding roots $r_2$ and $r_1$. Since $r_1^2(r_1-a_1)=1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}r_1^2(r_1-a_2)&=r_1^2(r_1-a_1)-(a_2-a_1)r_1^2\\\\r_1^2(r_1-a_2)&=1-(a_2-a_1)r_1^2\\\\r_1^2(r_1-a_2)&<1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $r_1\\le a_2$, then $r_2>a_2\\ge r_1$. Otherwise, the cubic for $a_2$ is below $y=1$ at $x=r_1$ and strictly increasing for $x>a_2$, so its intersection is to the right of $r_1$.\n\nTherefore $r_2>r_1$: the positive root increases as $a$ increases.",
